  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how permafrost ground temperatures and thaw depth are controlled by interacting climate, vegetation, snow, soil, water, and disturbance processes. In the landscape cross-section, warmer air temperatures are shown as a broad driver that increases permafrost temperature and deepens thaw. Snow cover acts as an insulator, while shrubs trap drifting snow and tend to warm the ground in winter. By contrast, dense tree canopies intercept snow and vegetation shades the surface and boosts evapotranspiration, which can cool the ground in summer.\n\nSeveral disturbances alter this balance. Infrastructure and wildfire are both depicted as modifying surface energy exchange by disturbing snow, the organic surface layer, and albedo. Ground moisture is another key control: ponded water delays freezing, warms the ground, thickens the active layer, and may promote talik formation. A wetter active layer also appears to enhance heat transfer through the soil, further increasing thaw thickness. The terrain cross-section highlights bedrock, ice-rich soil, the active layer above permafrost, and a transition from wetter to drier ground.\n\nThe small conceptual panel distills these controls into a vertical system linking atmosphere, vegetation, snow cover, organic layer, soil, and geothermal heat, with a “buffer zone” emphasizing the insulating middle layers.",
